      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <VentilationSystem>
VENTILATION - HEAT RECOVERY VENTILATION (HRV)

Your house has an existing Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) unit - a Venmar AVS Duo 1.4. The HRV was unplugged while I was there - so I suspect this HRV has not been used in awhile ? This HRV is actually quite efficient and it is the only means to ventilate the house as the rage hood fan does not vent out, and both bathrooms have HRV boost switches that don't work as the HRV is unplugged. It looks like the HRV boost switches in the bathrooms may be broken, however you can order new ones to replace them.

Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient Heat Recovery Ventilation  (HRV) unit is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality. Based upon the higher occupancy of this house, your house does indeed rely upon an HRV to manage indoor air quality.

If the current HRV cannot be fixed, I suggest you research replacing it with an ENERGY STARÂ® HRV, with a minimum 75% heat recovery efficiency rating and includes low electricity use ECM fan motors. Ideally, this new replacement system would be designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.

Savings: By installing an ENERGY STARÂ® rated HRV, with a minimum 75% efficiency and has ECM fan motors (as compared to the efficiency of the existing HRV), is estimated to reduce your energy costs by $ 68 per year and decrease your annual energy usage by 0.9 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>
Your house currently has a 121 litre oil hot water tank, that is direct vented out the side of the upper basement wall, however there is extensive "scorching" of the vinyl siding and this is not good.

As your house has direct access to the Hay River gas lines, I have modeled the opportunity to replace the oil hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher. This hot water system is direct vented using 2 x white PVC pipes and there are no high temps in the exhaust, so no further damage to your siding.

Savings: By replacing your current oil hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.95 EF, you could potentially save $ 185 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
 
To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
Your house currently has an 82.1% efficient oil furnace that is direct vented out the side of the upper basement wall. As your house has direct access to the Hay River gas lines, I have modeled an option for you to upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 96% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.

Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 96% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 698 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 14.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

Suspected Moisture in Kitchen Wall Below Window

While I was using my Infrared Camera, it appeared to identify an accumulation of moisture in the wall below the kitchen window on the right side of the house. I had your daughter Montana look at this with me, and I suspect the flashing around this window is not well done and is allowing rainwater to seep back into the wall, causing wet insulation and likely mold &amp; rotting wall sheathing. If you look closely at the wall space between that window and the backsplash of the kitchen counter, you will see the paint is bubbling and sagging (an indication of the high level of moisture in that wall).

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from t he GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 5.7 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment can cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current furnace &amp; hot water tank draw their combustion air from outside the house and are not susceptible to back-drafting combustion products into the house.

During my assessment of your home, I did see smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors in your home. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following ventilation:
        - Kitchen range hood does not vent to the outside
        - 2 x bathroom will only exhaust through the HRV (which is currently not working)
        - Clothes dryer
        - A Venmar Duo 1.4 HRV (this needs to be serviced &amp; used, including new bathroom boost switches)

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs - Hay River

The following Nov. 1, 2022 values, before GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:
Electricity - $17.77 min, $0.32 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.35 per kWh thereafter
Oil  - $1.32 per litre 
Propane - $0.82 per litre delivered, $3.78 per m3 piped to the house
Wood - $310.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested
Pellets - $4.86 per bag, $258.57 per tonne bulk delivered

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disclaimer
The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Your Energy Advisor

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.

Sincerely,

Ken Baigent, Contracted Senior Energy Management Specialist
Arctic Energy Alliance
Phone: (867) 920-3333
Fax: (867) 873-0303
E-mail: k.baigent@aea.nt.ca
